    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nSpacecraft Reaches Asteroid NASA Hit Hard in 2022 for the First-Ever Space Crash Site Investigation\n45 photos\nPhoto: ESA\nBack in September 2022, humanity managed to do something it had never done before: it slammed a speeding spacecraft into a distant asteroid on purpose, as it looked to prove that, if need be, we can change the trajectory of a celestial body that might head our way to end us. And now, another spacecraft has reached the target asteroid to perform what will be the first-ever crash site investigation performed in space.\nThe mission that impacted the piece of rock was run by American space agency NASA, and it was called the Double Asteroid Redirection Test (DART). It called on a spacecraft weighing around 1,280 pounds (580 kg) to slam into an asteroid called Dimorphos at a speed of about 14,000 mph (22,530 kph).\nThe research conducted in the aftermath of the strike confirmed that the impact was successful. It generated a plume of debris across 6,200 miles (10,000 km) of space, and slowed the orbit the Dimorphos asteroid followed around its bigger sibling, Didymos, by 33 minutes, a much greater change than predicted.\nThe historic mission was so important that it immediately triggered another one. This time run by the European Space Agency (ESA), it is meant to get to the site of the impact and have a look at it in a bid to answer questions about what the impact did to the asteroid itself, the crater it left behind, if any, and, of course, determine the composition of the rock to see how that affected the results of the strike.\nESA's mission is called Hera, and it launched in late 2024. It comprises the main spacecraft and two CubeSats called Milani and Juventas, which will be used to study Dimorphos from an up-close-and-personal position.\nPhoto: ESA The collection of hardware arrived at its target asteroids at the beginning of the month, a few weeks sooner than initially anticipated, following a two-year journey and 122 million miles (196.5 million km) traveled.\nNow in position just 18 miles (30 km) away from the binary system 65803 Didymos , the spacecraft is getting ready to do its thing. According to the Europeans, it has already begun the early characterization phase, which has it collecting key data on the two asteroids.\nHera's handlers here on Earth hope it will deliver the first clear images of what an asteroid so small (Dimorphos is just 495 feet, or 151 meters in diameter) looks like after an impact of this magnitude.\nCome December, Hera will move closer to the two asteroids, and it will begin the detailed characterization phase, which should give it even more insight into what happened and how the relationship between the two bodies has changed.\nPhoto: ESA Presently, the spacecraft flies around the common center of gravity of the two bodies, with the speed of a quick insect, moving in nearly straight rather than circular lines around the asteroids. For the phase coming next week, it will adopt a more complex bowtie-shaped hyperbolic orbit.\nEarly next year, Europeans will command Hera to move even closer for what is described as a crash site investigation. For this task, it will use the instruments it already has on board, and it will also deploy the two CubeSats it carries. These are tasked with actually landing on the surface of the asteroid to collect data.\nWhen it announced these details, ESA also revealed that Hera has already snapped images of the asteroids. For one reason or another, these have not been released yet, but we're promised that will happen as soon as Wednesday, November 25, when we are to expect to see the bigger Didymos asteroid in all its close-up glory.\nThe successful mission of the small-car-sized spacecraft should make it obvious to us whether we have the ability to change the trajectory of an asteroid that might one day impact our planet – a key step in planetary defense efforts against such threats.\nPhoto: ESA If the findings are as good as people expect, the kinetic impact method used by DART could become the main weapon against potential death from the sky. But even so, there are so many factors at play here that a touch of caution wouldn't hurt.\nDART hit a pretty small target, and there are much bigger near-Earth asteroids to worry about. Such a mission launched in a time of need would probably require much bigger spacecraft hitting their target much faster.\nOr, at least, as far out from Earth as possible so that a minor change in trajectory there would translate into a completely different path closer to home.\nExactly one year ago, ESA said there were around 40,000 near-Earth asteroids out there – at least, that's how many we know about. Of that, some 2,000 of them have a non-zero chance of impacting Earth sometime in the next hundred years, according to the agency. Very close to zero, true, but not actually zero...\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nhera Dart asteroid spacecraft WEEKEND\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT GTA 6 Will Certainly Want To Check Out This 1970 Ford, Only One Ever Made",
